The same `main.zig`, `Commands`, `Events` and web frontend build for Android and iOS. Oriel gives each platform a native shell around its own webview:

- **Android:** a thin Kotlin Activity hosting `android.webkit.WebView`, driven over JNI from `liboriel.so`. Every window is an Activity of its own, so in Android's desktop mode each one is a separate, resizable window.
- **iOS:** UIKit with a `WKWebView` per window, driven from Zig through the Objective-C runtime. There is no Xcode project and no Swift.

[`examples/showcase`](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/tree/main/examples/showcase) is one app that uses every feature and builds for all five platforms.

# Status

| | Android | iOS |
|---|---|---|
| Runs on | A Pixel phone (Tensor, Mali-G715), Android desktop mode (Mali-G925), the Android 15 emulator | The iOS simulator, in CI |
| Webview | `android.webkit.WebView` (Chromium) | `WKWebView` |
| Packaging | APK (sideloading) and AAB (Play), through a generated Gradle project | `.app` and `.ipa` |
| Local AI | ggml on the CPU (ARM dotprod / i8mm kernels), Vulkan, OpenCL (Adreno), Hexagon (Snapdragon NPU) | ggml on Metal |
| Status | Verified | Preview: not yet run on a device |

# Building

## Android

Needs a JDK 17, the Android SDK (platform-tools, Gradle 8.11.1+) and the NDK (`$ANDROID_NDK_HOME`).

```sh
oriel android init                 # android/: the Gradle project, generated from build.zig
oriel android dev --abi arm64      # debug build on the connected device, with the dev server
oriel android build --apk --aab    # release APK and AAB (arm64-v8a and x86_64)
```

Or with Zig directly: `zig build -Dtarget=aarch64-linux-android` writes `zig-out/jniLibs/arm64-v8a/liboriel.so`, which Gradle packages.

## App-owned Android extensions

Implement `OrielAndroidExtension` in app-owned Kotlin or Java sources to
configure WebViews, receive Activity and WebView lifecycle callbacks, handle
native results and permissions, or supply a custom file picker. Register those
classes with `.android.extensions` in `build.zig`; Oriel regenerates the
internal `OrielAppExtensions` registry on each Android build.

The [Android extensions guide]($link.page('docs/android-extensions')) covers
source registration, pinned Maven SDK dependencies, request-code namespaces,
and callback ownership. WebView hooks apply to the WebView renderer; Activity
hooks also run with native UI.

## iOS

Needs Apple's SDK: on a Mac, `xcrun --sdk iphoneos --show-sdk-path`; on Linux, [xtool](https://github.com/xtool-org/xtool) extracts it from `Xcode.xip`.

```sh
oriel ios build [--simulator] [--ipa]    # zig-out/ios/<Name>.app (and .ipa)
oriel ios dev --url http://<LAN address>:5173/
oriel ios install [--simulator]
```

`zig build check -Dtarget=aarch64-ios` type-checks without the SDK.

# What replaces the desktop-only parts

Phones have no tray, no menu bar and no system-wide hotkeys. On Android and iOS those modules are off by default, and turning one on is a build error that says what to use instead:

| Desktop | Android | iOS |
|---|---|---|
| Tray icon | A Quick Settings tile (`.android = .{ .tile = "…" }`), and actions on a foreground service's notification | — |
| System-wide hotkeys | In-app keyboard shortcuts (`global_shortcut`), listed in the system's Meta+/ helper | — |
| Typing into other apps (`input`) | The Oriel keyboard (`.android = .{ .input_method = "…" }`): `oriel.android.commitText` types into any app's focused field | — |
| Running in the background | A foreground service (`oriel.android.setForegroundService`), for example to keep recording | Background audio (`.ios = .{ .background_audio = true }`) |
| Updater | The stores | The App Store |

Android also sends *system events* (`oriel.android.onSystemEvent`): a tile tap, a notification action, the headset button, the keyboard's mic button, and `trim-memory`, the cue to free large models when the app goes to the background.

# Voice to text on phones

[Dictation]($link.page('docs/modules')) works on both platforms. On Android it has two engines:

- **Whisper**, offline in any language. Each model runs where Compare measured it faster on the device (the CPU until then): on a Pixel's Mali-G715, small on Vulkan and base on the CPU, with base drafting the live text at about 1.3 s per update.
- **The system recognizer**, with no model to download: on a Pixel, Google's on-device model, streaming about one update a second.

The showcase's "dictate anywhere" puts either into any app: tap the tile, the keyboard's mic or the headset button, talk, tap again, and the text lands in the focused field.

# Chat on phones

[Chat]($link.page('docs/modules')) runs a local LLM on the phone: Qwen2.5 0.5B and 1.5B or Llama 3.2 1B in q4_0, which ggml runs with its ARM dotprod/i8mm kernels. As with dictation, the CPU is the default on phones until Compare measures the GPU. The showcase's Chat tab has a mic that dictates the message.

# Also on Android

- **Deep links** open the app from a URL (`oriel-showcase://note/Hello` in the showcase) and reach `deep_link.onOpen`, cold or running.
- **Files** go through the Storage Access Framework. `dialog.openFile` returns a copy in the app's cache. Save with `dialog.writeFile(io, path, data)`, which writes through the picked document's descriptor.
- **Insets:** the page is kept clear of the status bar, the display cutout and the keyboard, and fills the window in desktop mode.
- **Models** live in the app's external files directory, where `adb push` can reach them.

The full notes, including what was verified on which device, are in [docs/android.md](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/blob/main/docs/android.md) and [docs/ios.md](https://github.com/highercomve/Oriel/blob/main/docs/ios.md).

# Google Play baseline

New Android projects compile and target API 36 (Android 16), with Android Gradle Plugin 8.13.2, Kotlin 2.3.21, and a Gradle 8.14.3 wrapper. Install `platforms;android-36` in the SDK. Android 10 remains the minimum device version. Existing generated projects keep edited Gradle files; update those settings or regenerate with `oriel android init --force` after saving your edits.
